  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 203 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nWednesday Through Thursday...\n\nBroad upper level southwest to zonal flow will set up through \nThursday, with a shortwave trof tracking along the northern CONUS. \nSimilar to the front over the area today, we will see another weak \nand slow-moving cold front approach the area Wednesday night through \nThursday. The main difference with this front is that we will see \nbetter llvl southwesterly flow ahead of this boundary, increasing \nmoisture availability. Thus, we will see increasing chances for\nshowers along the front, largely overnight Wednesday. Best \nchances will be along/north of a line from Sigourney to Dubuque \nIA (40-70%) with lesser chances for the remainder of the area \n(20-40%). Overall accumulation will be low and generally \n<0.10-0.25\" for all, with an isolated thunderstorm possible as \nwell. This activity will diminish Thursday AM, with decreasing \ncloud cover through the afternoon hours. Otherwise, daytime \ntemperatures will be in the low-mid 70s through this stretch, \nwith nights in the mid-upper 50s. \n\nFriday and Beyond...\n\nThe end of the week appears to be a similar set-up to the beginning \nof the week, with the region sandwiched between a low pressure over \nthe Central Plains and a high pressure over the Great Lakes. This \nwill support southeasterly flow across the CWA with a weak warm \nfront along the confluence zone. Continued advection of Pacific \nmoisture keeps PoPs (30-50% coverage) around Friday through Saturday \nnight. On Sunday, a longwave trough over the western CONUS is \nlikely to eject eastward into the Northern Rockies, paving the \nway for a more active pattern heading into the first week of \nNovember.",
